-- title:
--   Inertia order equals the supersingular place width
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $M \ge 5$ with $p \nmid M$, let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $p$ and $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K_1$ be the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of $\mathrm{x1x0FunctionFieldC}\ \mathbb{Q}\ M\ p$ (the $\mathbb{Q}$-field of $q$-expansions attached to $\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_0(p)$, i.e. adjoin of the ratios $\mathrm{intFormRatiosC}$ for that group), and $K_2$ the corresponding base change of $\mathrm{qExpFunctionFieldC}\ \mathbb{Q}\ \Gamma_0(Mp)$, with $K_2 \le K_1$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, containing $\zeta$ and with $p$ in its maximal ideal, which is generated by $\varpi$, together with compatible $A$-algebra structures on $K_1$ and $K_2$; let $j \in K_1$ and $j_2 \in K_2$ be the nonzero elements whose underlying Laurent series is the image of $\mathrm{jq}$ under $\mathrm{coeffEmb}$. Assume given a morphism $\pi_2$ of the two-chart integral models $\mathrm{TwoChartIntegralModel}\ A\ K_1\ j \to \mathrm{TwoChartIntegralModel}\ A\ K_2\ j_2$ and an $A$-algebra map $\iota_{F_2}$ of finite-chart algebras $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\ A\ K_2\ j_2 \to \mathrm{chartAlgFin}\ A\ K_1\ j$ compatible with the inclusions into $L((q))$, such that $\pi_2$ commutes with the structure morphisms to $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$, the square relating $\iota_{F_2}$ to the two finite-chart immersions $\mathrm{iFin}$ commutes, $\pi_2$ pulls the finite chart back to the finite chart, $\pi_2$ and $\iota_{F_2}$ are finite, and an element of $K_1$ lies in $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\ A\ K_1\ j$ exactly when it is integral over the image of $\iota_{F_2}$. Let $z$ be a point of the model at which the germ of the global section coming from $\varpi$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and $y$ a point of $\mathrm{XFin}\ A\ K_1\ j$ mapping to $z$ under $\mathrm{iFin}$, such that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $p$ and every ring map $\varphi$ from the finite chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel $y.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, the value $\varphi(\mathrm{jChartFin})$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\ p\ \Omega$, i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $p$-torsion point. Let $G$ be a finite group acting faithfully on $K_1$ by semiring automorphisms, acting trivially on the elements of $K_1$ lying in $K_2$ and with fixed elements exactly those lying in $K_2$, together with a compatible action on $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\ A\ K_1\ j$; put $\mathfrak{y} = y.\mathrm{asIdeal}$ and $\mathfrak{y}_2$ its contraction along $\iota_{F_2}$. Finally let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ all of whose elements satisfy $x^{p^n} = x$ for some $n > 0$, let $w$ be a place of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ M$ (a proper valuation subring containing $k$ and a principal ideal ring) lying in $\mathrm{ssPlaces}\ p\ M\ k$, and let $\rho : \mathrm{chartAlgFin}\ A\ K_2\ j_2 \to \mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ M$ be a ring map with $\rho(\varpi) = 0$, $\rho(\mathrm{jChartFin}) = \mathrm{jGeomGen}\ k\ M$, image inside the valuation subring of $w$, nonunit values exactly on $\mathfrak{y}_2$, and such that every element of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ M$ is a ratio of elements of the $k$-subalgebra generated by the image of $\rho$. Then the cardinality of the inertia subgroup $\mathfrak{y}.\mathrm{inertia}\ G$ equals $\mathrm{placeWidthChar}\ p\ M\ w$, namely $\mathrm{jWidthChar}\ p$ of the value of $w$ at $\mathrm{jGeomGen}\ k\ M$ divided by the ramification index $\mathrm{placeRamificationJ}\ M\ w$.
--
--   This identifies the order of the inertia group of the diamond-operator cover of the integral model at a supersingular point of the special fibre with the characteristic-$p$ width of the corresponding supersingular place of the level-$M$ modular function field; classically both sides compute $|\mathrm{Aut}(E_0, C_M)/\{\pm 1\}|$. It feeds the local analysis of the special fibre at supersingular points, being used to describe the completed stalk of the crossing model in terms of this inertia order.
